WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.760
Feedback explains how a winner pulls ahead.

2
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.660
Does it need help?

3
00:00:08.283 --> 00:00:10.383
What should a punishment signal look like?

4
00:00:11.183 --> 00:00:14.783
It should come from strongly supported neurons, and kill weak ones.

5
00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:17.253
It should spare strong ones.

6
00:00:18.033 --> 00:00:20.433
And it should start only after TrkA has risen.

7
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:23.793
The candidates were already on the gene list.

8
00:00:24.793 --> 00:00:28.153
Strongly supported neurons make BDNF and NT-4.

9
00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:33.593
Sympathetic neurons carry little of the usual receptor for these, TrkB.

10
00:00:34.413 --> 00:00:35.913
But they do carry p75.

11
00:00:37.897 --> 00:00:41.857
p75 belongs to the tumour necrosis factor receptor family.

12
00:00:42.757 --> 00:00:44.537
In this setting, it can promote death.

13
00:00:45.517 --> 00:00:49.357
These factors can act on neighbouring neurons, strong ones included.

14
00:00:50.297 --> 00:00:51.297
Why are the winners spared?

15
00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:01.710
A neuron with strong NGF and TrkA signaling is protected.

16
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:08.450
One likely reason: strong TrkA signaling keeps survival pathways high, such as AKT.

17
00:01:09.650 --> 00:01:12.290
That outweighs a death signal from p75.

18
00:01:13.270 --> 00:01:15.670
A weakly supported neuron has no such cushion.

19
00:01:16.733 --> 00:01:20.993
The lab tested three predictions in the mouse superior cervical ganglion.

20
00:01:21.983 --> 00:01:24.803
First, competing neurons differ in TrkA level.

21
00:01:25.623 --> 00:01:27.823
This was tested in ganglia lacking Bax.

22
00:01:28.867 --> 00:01:34.467
Second, neurons with low TrkA show high p75 death signaling, read out by

23
00:01:34.467 --> 00:01:35.687
phosphorylated c-Jun.

24
00:01:36.717 --> 00:01:38.817
c-Jun is a transcription factor.

25
00:01:39.617 --> 00:01:44.397
A stress kinase downstream of p75 helps activate it on the way to death.

26
00:01:45.367 --> 00:01:49.267
Third, removing punishment should delay the loss but not prevent it.

27
00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:55.487
Sketch neuron number against age, in normal mice and in mice lacking p75.

28
00:01:56.587 --> 00:01:57.927
What does the second curve do?

29
00:02:04.267 --> 00:02:06.027
Both start at similar numbers.

30
00:02:06.847 --> 00:02:09.927
The normal curve falls steeply in the first days after birth.

31
00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:14.070
The curve without p75 falls more slowly.

32
00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:16.590
So for a while, it has more neurons.

33
00:02:17.683 --> 00:02:20.823
The losers still die of too little NGF, through Bax.

34
00:02:21.763 --> 00:02:23.143
Only the extra push is gone.

35
00:02:24.133 --> 00:02:26.813
All three predictions held, including the delay.

36
00:02:27.883 --> 00:02:31.003
In the simulations, punishment made the split faster.

37
00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:33.543
Without it, weak neurons lingered.

38
00:02:34.517 --> 00:02:36.177
The endpoint is less settled.

39
00:02:37.057 --> 00:02:41.697
In the 2008 study, the null ganglion reached the normal number by six months.

40
00:02:42.767 --> 00:02:47.327
An earlier study from Freda Miller's laboratory also found the loss delayed.

41
00:02:48.067 --> 00:02:50.747
But it counted an excess that lasted into adulthood.

42
00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:53.550
So both found the loss delayed.

43
00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:57.430
They report different numbers at the latest ages counted.

44
00:02:58.483 --> 00:03:04.403
Takeaway. In the proposed model, winners speed the death of losers through p75.

45
00:03:05.523 --> 00:03:10.003
Without it, the loss is delayed; whether any neurons are permanently spared is less

46
00:03:10.003 --> 00:03:10.323
settled.
